---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = "Trademark Class Guide (Classes 1–45) | BRNDNUR Advisory LLP";
const description = "Understand the Nice Classification system for Indian trademark applications. Discover how goods and services are categorized and how to choose the right class.";
const canonical = "https://brndnur.com/resources/trademark-class-guide/";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  currentPath="/resources/trademark-class-guide/"
>
  <main id="main-content">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="container">
        <p class="eyebrow">Classification Guide</p>
        <h1>Trademark Class Guide: Understanding Classes 1 to 45.</h1>
        <p class="lead">How the Nice Classification system works in India, the critical distinction between goods and services, and how to select the right coverage for your business.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/trademark-classes/">View Class Directory &rarr;</a>
          <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/resources/">Back to Resources</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="container">
        <article class="legal-prose">
          <div class="legal-box">
            <strong>Key Principle:</strong> Trademark protection in India is class-specific. Registering a mark in Class 25 (Apparel) does not automatically protect the same name in Class 43 (Restaurants) or Class 35 (Retail & E-commerce).
          </div>

          <h2>1. What Is the Nice Classification?</h2>
          <p>India follows the Nice Classification, an international classification system administered by WIPO comprising 45 distinct classes:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Classes 1 to 34 (Goods):</strong> Cover physical, tangible products ranging from chemicals and pharmaceuticals to clothing, electronics, and packaged foodstuffs.</li>
            <li><strong>Classes 35 to 45 (Services):</strong> Cover business operations, digital services, hospitality, transportation, technology, and legal/personal services.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>2. Goods vs. Services: The Operational Distinction</h2>
          <p>Founders frequently confuse product manufacturing with retail or consultancy services:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>If you manufacture and bottle spices, your primary class is <strong>Class 30</strong> (Spices, seasonings, staples).</li>
            <li>If you also operate an online retail store or marketplace selling those spices, you also need <strong>Class 35</strong> (Online retail, advertising, business administration).</li>
            <li>If you operate a cloud-based software or SaaS platform, your software may fall under <strong>Class 9</strong> (Downloadable software) and <strong>Class 42</strong> (Software as a Service, cloud computing).</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>3. Popular Classes for Indian Startups</h2>
          <div class="card-grid" style="margin: 24px 0;">
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 5 &bull; Pharma & Supplements</h3>
              <p>Pharmaceuticals, veterinary preparations, dietary supplements, medical hygiene products.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 9 &bull; Tech & Electronics</h3>
              <p>Mobile apps, computer software, consumer electronics, telecommunications gear.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 25 &bull; Clothing & Footwear</h3>
              <p>Readymade garments, apparel, headwear, athletic wear, footwear.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 29 &bull; Dairy & Processed Foods</h3>
              <p>Meat, fish, poultry, preserved fruits, edible oils, milk and dairy products.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 30 &bull; Coffee, Bakery & Spices</h3>
              <p>Tea, coffee, flour, bread, confectionery, spices, seasonings, grains.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 35 &bull; Retail, Ads & Marketing</h3>
              <p>E-commerce stores, retail outlets, business management, digital marketing services.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 41 &bull; Education & Entertainment</h3>
              <p>Training institutes, digital courses, publishing, media production, events.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bn-card">
              <h3>Class 43 &bull; Food & Hospitality</h3>
              <p>Restaurants, cafes, food trucks, catering services, temporary accommodation.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2>4. Single Class vs. Multi-Class Applications</h2>
          <p>Indian trademark law allows filing single-class applications or multi-class applications under Form TM-A. Note that government statutory filing fees are calculated per class (₹4,500 per class for individuals/MSMEs; ₹9,000 per class for other corporate entities). Selecting only relevant commercial classes avoids unnecessary expenditure while securing necessary protection.</p>

          <h2>5. Common Classification Pitfalls</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Over-broad specifications that trigger examiner objections for vague descriptions.</li>
            <li>Omitting Class 35 when operating an e-commerce platform or multi-brand retail chain.</li>
            <li>Assuming that one registration protects every current and future business venture under the same company umbrella.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <div class="cta-panel" style="margin-top: 48px;">
          <p class="eyebrow">Need Help Choosing Classes?</p>
